<ClinicNote slug="concentric-eccentric-isometric">
  <p>Picture a bicep curl. The same muscle works in three different ways depending on what the weight is doing:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Concentric (lift):</b> the muscle shortens as it works. Curling the weight up, standing up from a chair, climbing stairs.</li>
    <li><b>Eccentric (lower):</b> the muscle lengthens under control, like a brake. Lowering the weight slowly, walking downstairs, landing from a jump.</li>
    <li><b>Isometric (hold):</b> the muscle works but doesn't change length. Holding the weight still, a wall sit, a plank.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Isometric: settle and build</h2>
  <p>Holds are often where rehab starts, because they load tissue without much movement. A small 2015 study found heavy holds eased knee-tendon pain for at least 45 minutes [1]. Larger reviews since then are more cautious:</p>
  <blockquote><p>"Isometric exercise does not appear to be superior to isotonic exercise in the management of chronic tendinopathy."</p><cite>Clifford et al., BMJ Open Sport Exerc Med, 2020 [2]</cite></blockquote>
  <p>So holds are a useful tool, not a magic switch. Bonus: a 2023 analysis of 270 trials found isometric training lowered resting blood pressure more than any other exercise type [7].</p>
  <h2>Eccentric: the brakes</h2>
  <p>Muscles can lower more weight than they can lift, which makes lowering work a strong strength builder [6]. It's also protective. The Nordic hamstring exercise, a slow lowering movement, is one of the best-supported injury-prevention exercises in sport:</p>
  <blockquote><p>"Programmes that include the NHE reduce hamstring injuries by up to 51%."</p><cite>van Dyk et al., Br J Sports Med, 2019 [3]</cite></blockquote>
  <h2>Concentric: the engine</h2>
  <p>Lifting is what gets you up hills, out of chairs and off the blocks. In real life it comes paired with lowering. Heavy, slow lifting and lowering together has matched eccentric-only programmes for knee and Achilles tendon pain, with people more satisfied and more likely to stick with it [4][5].</p>

